From 71a4243699c8da045d92e17da7ceae9e43e2ff4f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Iaroslav Voitovych <yaroslav.voytovych@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 17:50:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 0/5] Bluetooth: MGMT: stop and account mesh transmissions correctly

A mesh packet is advertised through a dedicated advertising instance
(le_num_of_adv_sets + 1). Since the list_empty() check was added to
mesh_send_done_sync(), that instance is what keeps hdev->adv_instances
non-empty, so a mesh transmission never disables advertising when its
done work runs or when it is cancelled: on the legacy path the
controller keeps advertising the packet and the instance stays
scheduled for 1000 s, with extended advertising the set stays enabled
for the duration the controller was given. The tools/mesh-tester cases
"Mesh - Send cancel - 1/2" encode the original behaviour and time out
on current kernels.

Independently, mesh_send_done_sync() completes the head of the pending
queue rather than the request that held the instance, so after
cancelling the in-flight handle the next, still unsent, packet is
reported complete without having been advertised.

Tearing the instance down means controller commands inside
mesh_send_done_sync(), and that work clears HCI_MESH_SENDING first: a
Mesh Send arriving while it waits for the controller sees the flag
clear and queues a start, and mesh_next() queues the head of the queue
again afterwards. Patch 1 therefore comes first: it keeps the flag set
until the next packet has been queued or the scheduler goes idle, and
makes that decision under hdev->lock, where mesh_send() tests the flag,
without holding the lock across a controller command. Patch 2 tears
the mesh instance down through hci_remove_advertising_sync() on both
advertising paths - that set only with extended advertising, so other
advertisers keep running - and keeps Advertising Removed from being
emitted for the internal instance. Patch 3 completes the request that
acquired the instance. Patch 4 fixes an older problem the new tests
found: if the controller is powered off while a packet is on air, its
done work cannot be queued, HCI_MESH_SENDING stays set and no later
Mesh Send is ever started; the work is now submitted so that it runs
on the closed device and hands over. Patch 5 only adds a comment and
can be dropped (see "Count" below).

Dependency. Patch 1's reasoning (a clear flag means nothing is pending)
needs mesh_send() to remove a request whose first hci_cmd_sync_queue()
failed; today its error path removes it under an inverted condition,
so such a rejected request stays pending and, with the series, can
later be started by mesh_next(). That cleanup has already been posted
by another author, "Bluetooth: MGMT: fix mesh_tx leak on
hci_cmd_sync_queue() failure"
(Hui Peng, 2026-09-19, https://lkml.iu.edu/2609.2/10074.html); it is not
in bluetooth or bluetooth-next as of 2026-10-04. This series assumes it
and does not include it; the order is for the maintainers to decide.
With fault injection on that allocation, the series alone leaves the
rejected handle listed by Read Mesh Features; with the cleanup applied
the handle is gone, is never started or completed, and the next
requests progress.

Count. The done work runs cnt * 25 ms after the start, while the
instance uses the adapter's advertising interval (1280 ms by default).
With the tear-down in place, a scanning client in the emulator receives
one advertising event for Count 1 and for Count 3 at the default
interval, two and four at a 20 ms interval; doc/mgmt-protocol.rst
defines Count as the number of times the packet is sent. Sizing the
deadline for cnt events at the configured interval would keep Count 1,
what bluetooth-meshd sends, at 25 ms and give three events for Count 3,
but would hold such a request for more than 2.5 s, and the existing
mesh-tester cases that send Count 3 and wait for the end within 2 s
time out with it. This series leaves the deadline as it is; patch 5
says so in the code. Those counts come from the emulator's model of an
advertising set, not from a radio. The tests verify command ordering,
ownership and teardown at the existing Count x 25 ms host deadline.
They do not establish the requested number of advertising events or
receiver delivery. The relationship between that deadline and the
configured advertising interval requires separate validation.

Stable. 71af682ba469 ("Bluetooth: mgmt: Dequeue pending mesh_send_sync
entries on cancel"), whose send_cancel() patch 1 builds on, is in
7.2.9, 6.18.55, 6.12.112, 6.6.158 and 6.1.189. On those tips the five
patches apply cumulatively (offsets only); no stable kernel was built or
run. The cleanup named above is needed there as well.

Testing, all in qemu with tools/test-runner on 08e90633377f with the
cleanup above applied locally, KASAN, lockdep and fault injection
enabled, and tools/mesh-tester extended by the BlueZ series "[PATCH
BlueZ 0/4] mesh-tester: mesh advertising lifecycle tests" (posted
separately):

  mesh-tester, 55 cases, KVM:           unpatched 18/55, patched 55/55
  the same without the cleanup:         53/55 (the two rejected-start
                                        cases fail as described above)
  50 Send cases, TCG + valgrind:        unpatched 13/50, patched 50/50
                                        (one tester-side valgrind error
                                        in both)
  cancel/tear-down/receiver cases x5:   80/80, 50/50, 20/20
  mgmt-tester, 503 cases:               501/503 patched and unpatched
                                        (the two Security Level Changed
                                        cases need the bluetooth-next
                                        event)
  mesh-tester under KCSAN (unpatched 18/55, patched 55/55), and the
  socket-close cases unpatched vs patched under KASAN and lockdep: no
  report from Bluetooth code on either kernel (KCSAN reports one race
  in the APIC timer calibration at boot, on both)

Kernel traces from kprobe events in the guest show the cases do what
their names say: with request 1's enable held, Cancel(1) runs right
after the released start and finds nothing to dequeue, request 1
completes before any tear-down and request 2 is started once; with
request 1's tear-down held, Cancel(2) dequeues the start that
mesh_next() had queued (hci_cmd_sync_dequeue() returns true, the start
completes with -ECANCELED) and the next request is started. On the
previous two-patch teardown/owner-matching revision, the hold test
observed the second request started twice. The ownership prerequisite
(patch 1) prevents that interleaving in the recorded runs.

Not covered: an accepted start that runs after power-off failed at once
with -EINVAL in the emulator; a real driver may fail it differently.
A start that fails after hci_add_adv_instance() still leaves the
instance behind, and with extended advertising the next power on
re-enables it with the completed request's packet. Unregistering with
pending requests leaves them allocated. Both predate this series and
are not changed by it. No dependency on the old queue-head completion
behavior was found in the inspected BlueZ client. Public searches did
not establish a non-BlueZ workaround. Compatibility with private
clients and dependence on accidental extra airtime remain unestablished;
the tests currently validate HCI lifecycle rather than requested
radio-event count.

Iaroslav Voitovych (5):
  Bluetooth: MGMT: hand mesh transmissions over under hdev->lock
  Bluetooth: MGMT: remove the mesh advertising instance when done
  Bluetooth: MGMT: complete the mesh transmission that owned the
    instance
  Bluetooth: MGMT: finish a mesh transmission that ends while powered
    off
  Bluetooth: MGMT: note what the mesh done deadline does to Count

